// A Python `re` -> JavaScript `RegExp` translator.
//
// Two jobs, both load-bearing:
//
//  1. Accept exactly what CPython accepts. `check_schema` runs the metaschema's
//     `format: "regex"` check, which is literally `re.compile(...)`, so a
//     pattern Python rejects must raise here too (the script then dies before
//     printing anything). `\p{L}` and `(?<n>x)` are valid JS but invalid Python;
//     `(?P<n>x)`, `[]]`, `a{`, `a*+` and `(?>a)` are the reverse.
//
//  2. Match exactly what CPython matches. The two engines disagree on more than
//     you would like:
//       $   Python also matches just before a single trailing newline
//       .   Python excludes only \n; JS also excludes \r,  ,
//       \d  Python is all of Unicode Nd; JS is [0-9]
//       \w  Python is Unicode alphanumerics; JS is [A-Za-z0-9_]
//       \s  Python includes \x1c-\x1f and \x85 but not
//       \b  Python's word set is Unicode; JS's is ASCII
//       ^$  under (?m) Python breaks only on \n; JS also breaks on \r
//
// Everything is rewritten into explicit constructs under the `u` flag, so the
// `m` and `s` flags are never handed to JS — their semantics are emitted inline.

